Below is the complete Cisco IOS configuration template for the IKEv2/IPsec FlexVPN tunnel between CML V1 and physical R2, using PSK authentication, static VTI on V1 and dynamic VTI on R2.
I've kept your actual IP addresses and passwords hidden. This is a reconstructed configuration for studying and rebuilding the VPN, rather than a verbatim export of your running configurations. Some IOS-specific syntax may vary.
1. Topology
2. Underlay connectivity
V1 configuration:
configure terminal

interface GigabitEthernet0/0
 ip address dhcp
 no shutdown
 exit

ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 <VMWARE-NAT-GATEWAY>
Verification:
show ip interface brief
show ip route
show dhcp lease
ping <VMWARE-NAT-GATEWAY>
ping <HOME-PUBLIC-IP>
traceroute <HOME-PUBLIC-IP>
On the AT&T gateway, ensure the required IKEv2 traffic can reach R2. Your existing VPN forwarding configuration may already provide this.
3. IKEv2 proposal
Configure on both V1 and R2.
crypto ikev2 proposal CML-PROPOSAL
 encryption aes-cbc-256
 integrity sha256
 prf sha256
 group 19
 exit
Purpose: Define the cryptographic algorithms protecting IKEv2 negotiation.
4. IKEv2 policy
Configure on both routers.
crypto ikev2 policy CML-POLICY
 proposal CML-PROPOSAL
 exit
Purpose: Select the IKEv2 proposal used during negotiation.
5. IKEv2 keyring
V1:
crypto ikev2 keyring CML-KEYRING
 peer R2
  address <HOME-PUBLIC-IP>
  pre-shared-key local <PSK>
  pre-shared-key remote <PSK>
  exit
 exit
R2:
crypto ikev2 keyring CML-KEYRING
 peer V1
  identity key-id CML-V1
  pre-shared-key local <PSK>
  pre-shared-key remote <PSK>
  exit
 exit
Purpose: Store authentication secrets and identify the peer. R2 uses a key ID in this example because V1 is behind NAT and its public source address may change.
6. AAA authorization
Configure on both routers, reusing existing AAA configuration where appropriate.
aaa new-model
aaa authorization network CML-AAA local
Be careful modifying AAA on a remotely managed router. Do not overwrite your existing SSH authentication settings.
7. IKEv2 authorization policy
Configure on both routers.
crypto ikev2 authorization policy CML-AUTH
 route set interface
 route accept any
 exit

Command
Purpose
route set interface
Advertise the local tunnel interface address.
route accept any
Accept routes advertised by the authenticated peer.

We previously investigated restricting route acceptance, but your older IOS offered only the any option under route accept. Further restriction was deferred.
8. IKEv2 profile
V1:
crypto ikev2 profile CML-IKEV2
 match identity remote key-id HOME-R2
 identity local key-id CML-V1
 authentication local pre-share
 authentication remote pre-share
 keyring local CML-KEYRING
 aaa authorization group psk list CML-AAA CML-AUTH
 exit
R2:
crypto ikev2 profile CML-IKEV2
 match identity remote key-id CML-V1
 identity local key-id HOME-R2
 authentication local pre-share
 authentication remote pre-share
 keyring local CML-KEYRING
 aaa authorization group psk list CML-AAA CML-AUTH
 virtual-template 2
 exit
Purpose: Combine peer identification, authentication, authorization and tunnel creation.
Command
Purpose
match identity remote
Identify the remote peer
identity local
Identify this router
authentication local
Local authentication method
authentication remote
Remote authentication method
keyring local
Reference the stored PSK
aaa authorization group
Apply authorization policy
virtual-template 2
Dynamically create a tunnel interface

9. IPsec transform set
Configure on both routers.
crypto ipsec transform-set CML-TS esp-aes 256 esp-sha256-hmac
 mode tunnel
 exit
Purpose: Define the algorithms protecting actual network traffic.
esp-aes 256 — Encryption.
esp-sha256-hmac — Integrity and authentication.
mode tunnel — Protect the entire original IP packet.
10. IPsec profile
Configure on both routers.
crypto ipsec profile CML-IPSEC
 set transform-set CML-TS
 set ikev2-profile CML-IKEV2
 exit
Purpose: Associate IPsec encryption settings with the IKEv2 negotiation settings.
Configuration dependency: the tunnel interface references the IPsec profile, which references the IKEv2 profile and transform set.
11. R2 — Loopback interface
interface Loopback100
 description CML-FLEXVPN-OVERLAY
 ip address <R2-OVERLAY-IP> 255.255.255.255
 exit
Purpose: Provide a stable overlay IP address for the dynamic tunnel interface.
12. R2 — Dynamic VTI
interface Virtual-Template2 type tunnel
 description CML-FLEXVPN-RESPONDER
 ip unnumbered Loopback100
 tunnel mode ipsec ipv4
 tunnel protection ipsec profile CML-IPSEC
 exit

Command
Purpose
type tunnel
Create a tunnel template
ip unnumbered
Borrow the loopback IP
tunnel mode ipsec ipv4
Use native IPsec tunnel mode
tunnel protection
Apply IPsec protection

When V1 connects, R2 automatically creates a Virtual-Access interface based on this template.
In our build, that interface was Virtual-Access3.
13. V1 — Static VTI
interface Tunnel100
 description FLEXVPN-TO-HOME-R2
 ip address <V1-OVERLAY-IP> 255.255.255.255
 tunnel source GigabitEthernet0/0
 tunnel destination <HOME-PUBLIC-IP>
 tunnel mode ipsec ipv4
 tunnel protection ipsec profile CML-IPSEC
 no shutdown
 exit

Command
Purpose
ip address
Local overlay address
tunnel source
Local underlay interface
tunnel destination
Remote underlay endpoint
tunnel mode
Encapsulation method
tunnel protection
Apply encryption

14. Routing
First, verify whether IKEv2 authorization dynamically installed the peer's overlay route.
show ip route
show ip route <PEER-OVERLAY-IP>
If additional remote networks need to be reached, you can configure static routing or dynamic routing.
Example on V1:
ip route <HOME-LAB-NETWORK> <SUBNET-MASK> Tunnel100
R2 also needs return routes to any networks behind V1. These can be learned dynamically rather than statically referencing a temporary Virtual-Access interface.
BGP will eventually handle network advertisement between the two environments.
